
  Relevant constraint I found: this app has no build step and no dependencies. [38;5;153mindex.html:13[39m loads [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with a plain
  [38;5;153m<script>[39m tag, and [38;5;153mpackage.json[39m has no [38;5;153mdependencies[39m and no scripts. A third-party SDK means adding npm plus a bundler
  (or a CDN tag and a vendor on their availability); a hand-rolled sink means no new tooling but you own batching,
  retry, and the receiving endpoint.

  - [1mExisting error/log service (Sentry, Datadog, LogRocket, etc.)[0m — buys batching, retry, source maps, grouping,
    dashboards, and release tracking on day one. Costs a vendor dependency, a build step or CDN tag, and their
    pricing/data-residency terms. Hardest to reverse: their SDK shape leaks into call sites unless you wrap it.
  - [1mOwn endpoint on your backend[0m — buys full control over retention and redaction, no vendor, no build step (plain
    [38;5;153mfetch[39m). Costs you writing batching, retry/offline handling, and the server side that receives and stores the logs —
    the server side is real work that isn't in this repo.
  - [1mConsole-structured now, sink behind an interface[0m — defers the choice without blocking the logger work. Costs a round
    trip: you still can't see production failures until a sink is wired.

  Cheap to change later either way, if we do it right: the call sites, as long as the logger is our own module and the
  sink sits behind a one-function interface. That wrapper is the thing that makes this decision reversible, so I'd build
  it regardless of your answer.

  My recommendation is an existing service. Batching, retry, and offline buffering are where hand-rolled browser logging
  quietly fails, and "users report it after the fact" is precisely the case that depends on delivery actually being
  reliable.
